Per clauses 9.1, 9.3.1, and 8.2.23:

- In a **plain NAS message**, octet 1 contains:
  - Bits 8–5: **EPS bearer identity** (if protocol discriminator = EPS session management) **or Security header type** (if protocol discriminator = EPS mobility management);  
  - Bits 4–1: **Protocol discriminator**.

- In a **security protected NAS message**, octet 1 contains:
  - Bits 8–5: **Security header type** (mandatory, per clause 9.3.1);  
  - Bits 4–1: **Protocol discriminator** (same as plain NAS).

Thus, the difference is:  
→ In plain NAS, bits 8–5 may be either *EPS bearer identity* (for ESM messages) or *Security header type* (for EMM messages);  
→ In security protected NAS, bits 8–5 are *always Security header type*, regardless of protocol discriminator (clause 8.2.23, table 8.2.23.1; clause 9.3.1).

Per clauses 5.1.3.2.1.1 and 5.1.3.2.3.1:

The transition from **5GMM-REGISTERED** to **5GMM-DEREGISTERED** is triggered by:

- **NW-initiated deregistration requested** (UE side: [1] §5.1.3.2.1.1; Network side: [2] §5.1.3.2.3.1.1);  
- **UE-initiated deregistration requested** (Network side only, [2]);  
- **Local deregistration** (UE side, [1]);  
- **Non-initial registration rejected (e.g., #3, #6, #7)** (UE side, [1]; Network side, [2]);  
- **SR rejected (e.g., #3, #6, #7)** (Network side, [2]);  
- **Implicit deregistration** (Network side, [2]).

Note: “Deregistration requested (power off)” triggers transition from *Any state* → 5GMM-DEREGISTERED ([1]), but power-off is a distinct case not requiring intermediate 5GMM-REGISTERED state.

Per clauses 5.4.1.2.4.5 (a) and 5.4.1.3.7 (e), upon first receipt of an **AUTHENTICATION FAILURE** message from the UE with **5GMM cause #71 “ngKSI already in use”**, the AMF shall:

1. **Perform necessary actions to select a new ngKSI** (TS 24.501 §5.4.1.2.4.5 a; §5.4.1.3.7 e);  
2. **Re-send the same EAP-request message** (for EAP-based auth) or **same 5G authentication challenge** (for 5G AKA) in a new **AUTHENTICATION REQUEST** message to the UE (§5.4.1.2.4.5 a; §5.4.1.3.7 e);  
3. **Start or restart timer T3560** (as per Figure 5.4.1.3.7.1 and §5.4.1.3.7 b, which applies to AUTHENTICATION REQUEST retransmission);  
4. **Not abort the procedure**, and may alternatively re-initiate the full EAP-based or 5G AKA procedure (§5.4.1.2.4.5 a NOTE 1; §5.4.1.3.7 e NOTE 3).

No AUTHENTICATION REJECT is sent — only a corrected AUTHENTICATION REQUEST with new ngKSI (and valid SQN/MAC for 5G AKA). The UE stops T3520 upon receiving this message (§5.4.1.2.4.5 a; §5.4.1.3.7 e).

The AMF sends the **NETWORK SLICE-SPECIFIC AUTHENTICATION COMMAND** message to initiate the network slice-specific authentication procedure (Clause 5.4.7.2.1).  

Associated actions:  
- The AMF sets the **EAP message IE** to the EAP-request message (generated by the AMF or provided by the AAA-S via the NSSAAF);  
- The AMF sets the **S-NSSAI IE** to the HPLMN S-NSSAI or SNPN S-NSSAI to which the EAP-request is related;  
- The AMF **sends the message and starts timer T3575 per S-NSSAI** (Clause 5.4.7.2.1).

Upon receiving an EAP-request message B from the SMF in the PDU session authentication and authorization procedure, the UE sends a **PDU SESSION AUTHENTICATION COMPLETE** message containing the corresponding EAP-response message (Clause 6.3.1.2.2, [1]).

The UE shall:
- Set the **EAP message IE** of the PDU SESSION AUTHENTICATION COMPLETE message to the EAP-response message (Clause 6.3.1.2.2, [1]a);  
- Transport the message using the NAS transport procedure (Clause 6.3.1.2.2, [1]);  
- Stop timer **T3590** upon sending (Clause 6.3.1.2.2, [1]; confirmed by Figure 6.3.1.1, [6]).  

This applies only for non-emergency PDU sessions (Clause 6.3.1.1, [6]).

The UE initiates **either**:

- the **SCG failure information procedure** (clause 5.7.3), *if* the inability to comply occurs with SCG-related configuration received over SRB3 *and* MCG transmission is not suspended;  
- **or** the **connection re-establishment procedure** (clause 5.3.7), *in all other cases where AS security has been activated and SRB2 + at least one DRB/MRB (or SRB2 for IAB) are setup*;  
- **or**, if AS security has *not* been activated, the UE performs **actions upon going to RRC_IDLE** (clause 5.3.11) with release cause *‘other’*;  
- **or**, if AS security *has* been activated but SRB2 and DRB/MRB (or SRB2 for IAB) are *not yet setup*, the UE goes to RRC_IDLE with release cause *‘RRC connection failure’*.

Per clause 5.3.5.8.2, **no partial application is allowed** (Note 2), and the specific procedure depends on the reception context (SRB1 vs. SRB3), deployment (EN-DC, NR-DC, standalone), ConditionalReconfiguration status, and AS security/bearer setup state.

The message sent from the UE to the Network in the **general UL information transfer procedure** is **`ULInformationTransfer`**, as defined in Clause 5.7.2.1 and illustrated in Figure 5.7.2.1.

Additionally, specialized variants exist for specific contexts:
- **`ULInformationTransferMRDC`** for MR-DC dedicated information (Clause 5.7.2a.1, Figure 5.7.2a.1);
- **`ULInformationTransferIRAT`** for inter-RAT (e.g., E-UTRA) information transferred to NR MCG (Clause 5.7.2b.1, Figure 5.7.2b.1);
- **`ULDedicatedMessageSegment`** for segmented UL DCCH messages (Clause 5.7.7.1, Figure 5.7.7.1).

However, per the question’s scope — *“the UL information transfer procedure”* (generic, unqualified) — the base message name is **`ULInformationTransfer`** (Clause 5.7.2.1).

The primary purpose of the procedure depicted in the figure (Clause 5.8.3.1) is to inform the network that the UE:  
a) is interested or no longer interested to receive or transmit NR sidelink communication;  
b) is requesting assignment or release of transmission resources for NR sidelink communication;  
c) is reporting QoS parameters and QoS profile(s) related to NR sidelink communication;  
d) is reporting detection of a sidelink radio link failure or sidelink RRC reconfiguration failure;  
e)–l) as specified in clauses 5.8.3.1(a)–(l) — including reporting peer UE capability, RLC mode, sidelink DRX configuration/assistance information, groupcast/broadcast Destination L2 ID & QoS, DRX configuration reject, and U2N relay parameters.  

This is achieved via the **SidelinkUEInformationNR** message sent by the UE to the network.

The purpose of the procedure involving **SidelinkUEInformationNR** is to inform the network that the UE:  
a) is interested or no longer interested in receiving or transmitting NR sidelink communication;  
b) is requesting assignment or release of transmission resources for NR sidelink communication;  
c) is reporting QoS parameters and QoS profile(s) related to NR sidelink communication;  
d) is reporting that a sidelink radio link failure or sidelink RRC reconfiguration failure has been detected;  
e) is reporting the sidelink UE capability information of the associated peer UE for unicast communication;  
f) is reporting the RLC mode information of the sidelink data radio bearer(s) received from the associated peer UE for unicast communication;  
g) is reporting the sidelink DRX configuration received from the associated peer UE for NR sidelink unicast communication;  
h) is reporting the sidelink DRX assistance information received from the associated peer UE for NR sidelink unicast communication;  
i) is reporting, for NR sidelink groupcast or broadcast communication, additional information (FFS);  
j) is reporting, for NR sidelink groupcast or broadcast communication, the Destination Layer-2 ID and QoS profile associated with its interested services that sidelink DRX is applied;  
k) is reporting DRX configuration reject information from its associated peer RX UE, when the UE is a TX UE performing sidelink operation with resource allocation mode 1;  
l) is reporting parameters related to U2N relay operation.  

*(Per Clause 5.8.3.1 — General SIB12 acquisition SidelinkUEInformationNR)*

Per Clause 5.8.3.2, the UE triggers transmission of `SidelinkUEInformationNR` in RRC_CONNECTED upon any of the following events:

- Successful RRC connection establishment or resumption;  
- Change in interest (to receive/transmit NR sidelink communication, discovery, or U2N relay);  
- Change in QoS profiles;  
- Reception of `UECapabilityInformationSidelink` from the associated peer UE;  
- Update of RLC mode information from the associated peer UE;  
- Change to a PCell providing SIB12 including `sl-ConfigCommonNR`;  
- Declaration of sidelink radio link failure (per Clause 5.8.9.3) or sidelink RRC reconfiguration failure (per Clause 5.8.9.1.8);  
- Acceptance of a sidelink DRX configuration from the associated peer UE (for unicast);  
- Reception of sidelink DRX assistance information from the associated peer UE (for unicast, resource allocation mode 1);  
- For an RX UE: becoming interested (or no longer interested) in a groupcast/broadcast service subject to sidelink DRX — triggering reporting of Destination Layer-2 ID and QoS profile;  
- Change in configured sidelink reception/transmission frequencies (vs. last `SidelinkUEInformationNR`);  
- Change in `sl-TxResourceReqList`, `sl-RxInterestedFreqList`, `sl-TxResourceReqListDisc`, or `sl-RxInterestedFreqListDisc` content vs. last transmission;  
- Meeting U2N Relay/Remote UE threshold conditions (Cl. 5.8.14.2 / 5.8.15.2) when SIB12 includes corresponding relay config (e.g., `sl-RelayUE-ConfigCommon`).  

The trigger always results in initiating the procedure per Cl. 5.8.3.1 and populating the message per Cl. 5.8.3.3.

Per Clause 5.8.5 (Sidelink synchronisation information transmission for NR sidelink communication) and Figure 5.8.5.1:

The network-initiated sequence is:
1. **SIB12 acquisition** — Network broadcasts SIB12 containing sidelink configuration (e.g., `sl-ConfigCommonNR`, `sl-FreqInfoList`) on the NR cell;
2. **RRCReconfiguration** — Network sends dedicated RRC reconfiguration (e.g., `RRCReconfiguration` with `sl-ConfigDedicatedNR`) to configure UE-specific sidelink parameters;
3. **SLSS & MasterInformationBlockSidelink** — Network transmits SLSS and MIB-Sidelink (via PSS/SSS-like physical layer signals) for UE synchronization and basic sidelink system information.

This sequence applies to NR sidelink communication in (partial) coverage (Clause 5.8.5, Figure 5.8.5.1). For V2X sidelink, SIB13/SIB14 replaces SIB12 (Clause 5.8.5a.1).

The UE sends the **SidelinkUEInformationNR** message to indicate a failure during the sidelink RRC reconfiguration procedure.

Per **Clause 5.8.3.3**, when a sidelink RRC reconfiguration failure has been declared (as specified in Clause 5.8.9.1.8), the UE shall:

> 4 > if a sidelink radio link failure or a sidelink RRC reconfiguration failure has been declared, according to clauses 5.8.9.3 and 5.8.9.1.8, respectively;  
> 5 > include sl-FailureList and set its fields as follows for each destination for which it reports the NR sidelink communication failure:  
> 6 > set sl-DestinationIdentity to the destination identity configured by upper layer for NR sidelink communication transmission;  
> 6 > else if RRCReconfigurationFailureSidelink is received:  
> 7 > set sl-Failure as configFailure for the associated destination for the NR sidelink communication transmission;

This message is submitted to lower layers per Clause 5.8.3.2 (Initiation) and Clause 5.8.3.3 (Actions), and is the defined mechanism for reporting sidelink RRC reconfiguration failure to the network.

Note: There is no dedicated “SidelinkRRCReconfigurationFailure” message — the failure is reported *within* **SidelinkUEInformationNR**, via `sl-FailureList` with `sl-Failure = configFailure`.
